            <ArticleTitle>p-cresol sulfate and indoxyl sulfate: some clouds are gathering in the uremic toxin sky.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1323-1324</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0085-2538(17)30498-2</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.kint.2017.06.029</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>An increasing body of experimental and clinical evidence suggests that p-cresol sulfate and indoxyl sulfate contribute to the high cardiovascular burden in patients with chronic kidney disease. In a post hoc analysis on the HEMO trial, Shafi et al. failed to confirm an association between total p-cresol sulfate and indoxyl sulfate and cardiovascular outcomes in dialysis patients. Analytical issues and case-mix may explain the discrepant findings.</AbstractText>
